    {
      "program_name": "ECM Remodeling",
      "description": "This gene program drives extensive remodeling of the extracellular matrix to facilitate glioblastoma invasion. Tumor cells upregulate ECM-degrading enzymes and matrix-modifying activities, while increasing expression of cell-matrix adhesion molecules. Key components include a secreted protease (ADAMTS5) that cleaves brain-specific proteoglycans, enzymes for glycosaminoglycan synthesis (XYLT1) and sulfation (CHST8) that alter matrix composition, and receptors like CD44 and integrin-β4 that anchor cells to matrix substrates. Collagens and neural ECM glycoproteins (e.g., tenascin-R) are also present or induced. Collectively, these genes enable glioblastoma cells to break down structural barriers and interact with a permissive matrix, enhancing cell motility through brain tissue. Established studies show that ADAMTS5 is highly expressed in GBM cells and promotes invasion by cleaving brevican in the brain ECM ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16133547/#:~:text=glioblastoma%20tissues%20compared%20to%20control,through%20the%20cleavage%20of%20brevican)). CD44, a hyaluronan receptor, is enriched in invasive glioma stem-like cells and orchestrates signaling pathways that promote motility under hypoxic conditions ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10572085/#:~:text=High%20invasiveness%20is%20a%20characteristic,1%CE%B1%2C%20which%20then%20activates)). Upregulated ECM modifiers like XYLT1 can drive pro-invasive signaling (NF-κB activation) via aberrant proteoglycan glycosylation ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39992715/#:~:text=In%20this%20study%2C%20we%20found,results%20illustrate%20that%20proteoglycan%20modification)). Integrin β4, a laminin-binding integrin, is likewise elevated in high-grade gliomas, correlating with poor prognosis and linking tumor cells to the surrounding matrix and stromal cells ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10764336/#:~:text=in%20comprehensive%20therapies%2C%20including%20radiotherapy%2C,that%20ITGB4%20is%20upregulated%20in)). Altogether this program endows glioblastoma cells with an aggressive, invasive phenotype by dynamically remodeling the tumor microenvironment’s structural matrix.",
