      "predicted_cellular_impact": [
        "Enhanced extracellular matrix degradation and remodeling to facilitate tumor cell invasion",
        "Increased cell-matrix adhesion and motility through upregulated integrin and CD44 signaling"
      ],
      "evidence_summary": "Multiple genes in this program function cooperatively to remodel the brain extracellular matrix and promote invasive growth of glioblastoma cells. For example, the metalloprotease ADAMTS5 is significantly overexpressed in GBM and cleaves the neural proteoglycan brevican, thereby loosening matrix constraints and stimulating tumor cell invasion ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16133547/#:~:text=glioblastoma%20tissues%20compared%20to%20control,through%20the%20cleavage%20of%20brevican)). Concurrently, the hyaluronan receptor CD44 is highly expressed on invasive glioma stem-like cells, linking to pathways that drive cell migration especially under hypoxic stress ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10572085/#:~:text=High%20invasiveness%20is%20a%20characteristic,1%CE%B1%2C%20which%20then%20activates)). Glycosyltransferases such as XYLT1, which initiates proteoglycan GAG chain synthesis, are upregulated and have been implicated in cancer metastasis via NF-κB activation ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39992715/#:~:text=In%20this%20study%2C%20we%20found,results%20illustrate%20that%20proteoglycan%20modification)), suggesting aberrant matrix proteoglycan production can feed back to tumor signaling. Adhesion receptors like integrin β4, a laminin-binding integrin, are also elevated in gliomas; its increased expression correlates with tumor progression and an immunosuppressive microenvironment ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10764336/#:~:text=in%20comprehensive%20therapies%2C%20including%20radiotherapy%2C,that%20ITGB4%20is%20upregulated%20in)). These coordinated alterations enable tumor cells to dynamically alter and engage the surrounding stroma, fostering greater invasiveness.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "reference": "Nakada M. et al., Acta Neuropathol, 2005",
          "id": "DOI:10.1007/s00401-005-1032-6",
          "type": "DOI",
          "notes": "First identification of ADAMTS-5 overexpression in glioblastoma, demonstrating that ADAMTS-5 in GBM cells cleaves the ECM proteoglycan brevican and significantly increases tumor invasiveness ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16133547/#:~:text=glioblastoma%20tissues%20compared%20to%20control,through%20the%20cleavage%20of%20brevican))"
        },
        {
          "reference": "Inoue A. et al., Cancers, 2023",
          "id": "DOI:10.3390/cancers15194898",
          "type": "DOI",
          "notes": "Comprehensive review on CD44 in GBM, noting that CD44-high glioma stem cells are highly invasive and that CD44 signaling promotes tumor cell migration and proliferation, especially under hypoxic conditions ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10572085/#:~:text=High%20invasiveness%20is%20a%20characteristic,1%CE%B1%2C%20which%20then%20activates))"
        },
        {
          "reference": "Han J. et al., Cancer Res, 2025",
          "id": "PMID:39992715",
          "type": "PMID",
          "notes": "Reports that XYLT1, an enzyme initiating glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, is upregulated in metastatic tumors and activates NF-κB signaling, thereby promoting cancer cell survival and metastasis ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39992715/#:~:text=In%20this%20study%2C%20we%20found,results%20illustrate%20that%20proteoglycan%20modification)) (demonstrating the pro-invasive impact of ECM glycan modifications)"
        },
        {
          "reference": "Chen P. et al., Sci Rep, 2024",
          "id": "DOI:10.1038/s41598-023-49801-y",
          "type": "DOI",
          "notes": "Shows that ITGB4 (integrin β4) is upregulated in diffuse gliomas and associated with poorer prognosis and an immune-interactive tumor microenvironment ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10764336/#:~:text=in%20comprehensive%20therapies%2C%20including%20radiotherapy%2C,that%20ITGB4%20is%20upregulated%20in)), suggesting that increased integrin-mediated matrix attachment contributes to glioma aggressiveness"
        }
      ],
