    {
      "program_name": "Neural Connectivity",
      "description": "This program reflects the aberrant activation of neurodevelopmental and synaptic pathways in malignant glioblastoma cells. It includes multiple cell adhesion and guidance molecules normally involved in neuronal circuit formation, suggesting that tumor cells may mimic neuron-like interactions. Key genes encode axon guidance receptors and ligands (UNC5D, RGMA) that are typically active in directing neural cell migration and wiring, as well as synaptic adhesion molecules (MDGA2) and ion channel regulators (KCNIP1) associated with neuronal excitability. The presence of these genes indicates that glioblastoma cells can engage in neuron-like signaling and network integration. Indeed, recent studies have shown that glioma cells form functional synaptic connections with neurons, allowing direct neuron-to-tumor excitation that drives tumor growth and invasion ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7850064/#:~:text=The%20recent%20discovery%20of%20synaptic,cycle%20of%20enhanced%20neuronal%20activity)). Genes such as UNC5D (a netrin receptor) and RGMA (a repulsive guidance cue) hint that glioma cells exploit axon guidance pathways to infiltrate along neural tracts or establish tumor microtube networks reminiscent of axons ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7850064/#:~:text=which%20is%20supported%20by%20ample,it%20has%20been%20shown%20that)). Similarly, the synaptic adhesion factor MDGA2, which modulates formation of excitatory synapses in normal brain, may contribute to atypical synapse-like contacts between tumor cells or with neurons. The immediate-early gene ARC, typically induced by neuronal activity, is also present, suggesting that some glioblastoma cells undergo activity-dependent gene expression changes analogous to neurons. Collectively, this neural connectivity program implies that a subset of glioblastoma cells adopts neuron-mimicking behaviors to integrate into neural circuits and promote tumor progression.",
      "atomic_biological_processes": [
        {
          "ontology_id": "GO:0007411",
          "name": "axon guidance",
          "ontology_label": "biological_process",
          "citation": [
            {
              "reference": "Venkataramani V. et al., Neuro Oncol, 2020",
              "id": "DOI:10.1093/neuonc/noaa158",
              "type": "DOI",
              "notes": "Describes that glioma cells exploit neurodevelopmental pathways and extend tumor microtubes analogous to axons and dendrites, enabling brain invasion and intercellular network formation ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7850064/#:~:text=which%20is%20supported%20by%20ample,it%20has%20been%20shown%20that))"
            }
          ],
