      "predicted_cellular_impact": [
        "Formation of pseudo-synaptic contacts between tumor cells and neurons, fueling tumor growth",
        "Guided invasion of glioma cells along neural pathways via reactivation of axon guidance mechanisms"
      ],
      "evidence_summary": "Several genes in this program indicate that glioblastoma cells can co-opt neuronal communication pathways. For instance, UNC5D (a netrin receptor) and RGMA (a guidance cue) are usually involved in axonal pathfinding and neuronal positioning; their expression in tumor cells suggests the use of developmental guidance cues to navigate the brain parenchyma. This aligns with recent observations that glioma cells extend long tumour microtubes similar to neurites to infiltrate the brain and interconnect with each other ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7850064/#:~:text=which%20is%20supported%20by%20ample,it%20has%20been%20shown%20that)). Additionally, the presence of synapse-associated molecules like MDGA2 and ARC implies that tumor cells may engage in synaptic-like interactions. In fact, neuron-to-glioma synapses have been demonstrated, wherein glutamatergic signals from neurons directly stimulate glioma cell firing and proliferation ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7850064/#:~:text=The%20recent%20discovery%20of%20synaptic,cycle%20of%20enhanced%20neuronal%20activity)). Such direct integration into neural circuits represents a 'hijacking' of synaptic transmission by the tumor. Overall, the evidence supports that this gene program confers a pseudo-neuronal phenotype, enabling glioblastoma cells to receive neurotrophic signals and possibly enhancing their invasive growth and therapy resistance in the brain’s neural network.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "reference": "Venkataramani V. et al., Neuro Oncol, 2020",
          "id": "DOI:10.1093/neuonc/noaa158",
          "type": "DOI",
          "notes": "Reviews the concept of neuron-glioma synaptic communication, summarizing that glioma cells form functional glutamatergic synapses and tumor microtube networks which promote tumor progression and invasion in the brain ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7850064/#:~:text=The%20recent%20discovery%20of%20synaptic,cycle%20of%20enhanced%20neuronal%20activity)) ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7850064/#:~:text=which%20is%20supported%20by%20ample,it%20has%20been%20shown%20that))"
        },
