    {
      "program_name": "Hypoxic Response",
      "description": "This gene program encompasses cellular adaptations to the hypoxic and stressful microenvironment of glioblastoma, including mechanisms for metabolic reprogramming, oxidative stress resistance, and immune evasion. Many genes here are induced or activated under low oxygen or nutrient conditions and collectively promote tumor cell survival and aggressiveness. For example, LUCAT1 is a long non-coding RNA strongly upregulated by HIF-1α in hypoxic glioma stem cells, where it forms complexes that enhance HIF-1 target gene activation ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11300024/#:~:text=A%20new%20isoform%20of%20Lucat1,Depletion%20of%20LUCAT1%20impaired%20GSC)). This supports tumor adaptation to oxygen deprivation by sustaining stemness and growth even in poorly vascularized regions. Similarly, SLC7A11 (xCT) is a cystine transporter that elevates glutathione synthesis, protecting cells from oxidative damage and ferroptosis; its overexpression in glioblastoma is known to confer resistance to chemotherapy and oxidative stress by boosting antioxidant defenses ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27658422/#:~:text=resistance%20to%20oxidative%20stress%20and,and%20resistance%20to%20standard%20chemotherapy)). This program also includes factors influencing metabolism and ROS homeostasis, such as PPARGC1A (PGC-1α), a master regulator of mitochondrial biogenesis and oxidative metabolism whose high expression in glioblastoma is linked to increased energy production and poorer patient prognosis ([pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30578297/#:~:text=involved%20in%20tumor%20growth%20and,1%CE%B1%20displayed%20ROS)). Concurrently, genes associated with immune evasion are present: NLRC5, a transcriptional activator of MHC class I, is targeted for downregulation by cancers to avoid immune detection ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4889388/#:~:text=Tumor%20antigen%20presentation%20to%20CD8,cell%20activation)), and the H1 histamine receptor (HRH1), which in glioblastoma can drive immunosuppressive effects in the tumor microenvironment (high HRH1 correlates with T-cell dysfunction and worse survival ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11770814/#:~:text=Histamine%2C%20a%20key%20mediator%20of,open%20up%20a%20new%20avenue))). The astrocytic marker GFAP is also included, reflecting a reactive, stress-responsive glial state. Altogether, this program equips glioblastoma cells to withstand hypoxic and oxidative stress while modulating the immune environment to their advantage, leading to more aggressive, treatment-resistant tumor behavior.",
      "atomic_biological_processes": [
        {
          "ontology_id": "GO:0001666",
          "name": "response to hypoxia",
          "ontology_label": "biological_process",
          "citation": [
            {
              "reference": "Huang H. et al., Neuro Oncol, 2024",
              "id": "DOI:10.1093/neuonc/noae036",
              "type": "DOI",
              "notes": "Identifies LUCAT1 as a hypoxia-inducible lncRNA in glioblastoma; under low oxygen, HIF-1α induces LUCAT1, which then enhances expression of hypoxia-responsive genes, promoting GSC survival in hypoxia ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11300024/#:~:text=A%20new%20isoform%20of%20Lucat1,Depletion%20of%20LUCAT1%20impaired%20GSC))"
            }
          ],
          "Genes": [
            "LUCAT1",
            "EEPD1",
            "PLA2G4A",
            "GFAP"
          ]
        },
        {
          "ontology_id": "GO:0050777",
          "name": "negative regulation of immune response",
          "ontology_label": "biological_process",
          "citation": [
            {
              "reference": "Yoshihama S. et al., PNAS, 2016",
              "id": "DOI:10.1073/pnas.1602069113",
              "type": "DOI",
              "notes": "Shows that loss or epigenetic silencing of NLRC5 in cancers leads to reduced MHC-I expression and impaired CD8+ T cell activation, illustrating a mechanism by which tumors like GBM can evade immune surveillance ([pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4889388/#:~:text=Tumor%20antigen%20presentation%20to%20CD8,cell%20activation))"
            }
          ],
